"""Narrative of deep-dive report D4 (PPO in the plant); result sections are filled after evaluation."""
from pathlib import Path

from experiments.d5_extras import LABELS, training_curves

ARM_LABELS = dict(LABELS)
METHODS = [("pd_shared", "PD, shared gains (v2)"), ("pd_residual_acmed_s02_lowrate", "PD + residual, actor-critic (warm start)")]
_runs = []
for run in sorted(Path("runs/d5").glob("*-s*")) if Path("runs/d5").exists() else []:
    if run.is_dir() and (run / "best.pt").exists() and not run.name.startswith("killed"):
        name = run.name.split("-s")[0]
        if name not in dict(METHODS):
            METHODS.append((name, ARM_LABELS.get(name, name)))
        _runs.append(str(run))
TRAINING_SECTION = ("<h2>Training</h2>\n<p>One line per run. Validation every 20 updates on 14 fixed protocol v2 cases in the corrected plant.</p>\n" + training_curves(_runs)) if _runs else ""
_videos = Path("research/reports/10-dd-ppo/data/videos.html")
import json as _json

from experiments.d_report import wins_table

_metrics = Path("runs/d5/metrics_test.json")
WINS_SECTION = ("<h2>Where the learned controllers win and lose</h2>\n<p>Curated from the test tables: every (group, family, metric) with a significant paired difference against the protocol v2 PD, for the D3 residual (the warm start), the contact-family PPO arm (v2) and the all-family PPO arm v3 (three seeds pooled). Lower is better for every metric listed.</p>\n"
                + wins_table(_json.loads(_metrics.read_text()), [("pd_shared", "PD"), ("pd_residual_acmed_s02_lowrate", "residual"), ("ppo_contact_v2", "PPO contact"), ("ppo_all_v3", "PPO v3")])) if _metrics.exists() else ""
VIDEO_SECTION = ("<h2>Representative episodes</h2>\n<p>One case per family (median composite of the protocol v2 PD); contact families also at quarter speed.</p>\n" + _videos.read_text()) if _videos.exists() else ""

NARRATIVE = {
    "erratum": """<p><strong>Erratum 2 (2026-11-16, blocked family).</strong> The blocked-reference generator tested 'inside the surface' with the wrong sign for the table surface (60% of the blocked references, ADR 0007): those references had empty blocked and release phases, so their training windows in reports D2 to D4 contained no block at all (they started at the end of the reference), and their recovery metric was undefined and counted as zero in the composite. The generator is fixed, the reference pools were rebuilt and the test tables of this report were regenerated from the corrected cases. For the tuned PD the blocked composite moves from 7.10 to 7.62, for the warm start from 6.94 to 8.51, for the contact-family arm v2 from 6.17 to 7.81 and for seed 2 of v3 from 6.71 to 7.17; the recovery after release is 0.37 to 0.85 s for the learned controllers against 0.33 s for the PD. Finding 3's statement that the contact-family arm beats the PD on blocked targets is withdrawn: it beats its warm start and is level with the PD. Findings 5 and 7 gain a cause: in every D2 to D4 arm, 60% of the blocked training windows contained no block, so the blocked behaviour was under-represented in the data as well as in the reward.</p>
<p><strong>Erratum 3 (2026-11-16, wall surface).</strong> In the wall branch of the blocked and contact families the tool pointed 50 to 70 degrees below horizontal with targets up to 8 cm inside the wall, so the hand and the forearm reached the wall before the tool: link contact during 65 to 85% of the blocked phase, and in three of seven wall validation cases the tool never touched the wall. Those cases measured unsensed link collisions. The generators now tilt the tool 30 to 45 degrees, place wall targets 3 to 6 cm deep and reject any reference whose deepest pose brings a link within 2 cm of the wall (ADR 0007); PD rollouts confirm tool contact for 93 to 98% of the blocked phase and no link contact. The tables of this report were regenerated a second time from the corrected wall cases: PD 9.09, warm start 10.23, contact-family arm v2 8.71 and seed 2 of v3 8.21 on the blocked composite. Every D2 to D4 arm was trained with the flawed wall references, which bears on their blocked and contact rows.</p>""",
    "question": """<p>Reports D2 and D3 trained the controllers by first-order methods through simulator or learned-model gradients. The best of them, a PD + residual trained by a short-horizon actor-critic through median-clipped finite differences with a light torque-rate penalty, beats the tuned PD on every family and held-out group except the deep blocked target, where it pushes as hard as the PD; the arms that did yield there tracked worse in free space. Can reinforcement learning in the plant, which needs no gradient through contact and can optimise a long-horizon objective with a hard force limit, get both: the tracking of the warm start and compliance on targets that cannot be reached? What does the warm start contribute, and which training families does a controller need to see?</p>""",
    "setup": """<p><strong>Medium.</strong> The batch of MuJoCo plant simulators of the deep dive, forward only: protocol v2 training windows of 2 s with per-window robot instances, payloads of 0 to 3 kg and 10 N pulses, 64 windows per update (ADR 0006).</p>
<p><strong>Controller and actor.</strong> PD + residual with the network and two-scale 1 kHz proprioceptive history of report D3. The network decides every 10 ms and its residual is held in between while the history is still sampled every millisecond, so the weights of the 1 kHz warm start keep the meaning of their inputs (on the warm start the decision period makes no measurable difference between 1 and 20 ms). The actor is a diagonal Gaussian in the pre-tanh residual space (residual = half the torque limit times tanh of the action), initial standard deviation 0.1, learned; the deterministic policy exported for evaluation is the mean.</p>
<p><strong>Critic.</strong> The privileged critic of the actor-critic run (state, touch force, payload, 500 ms reference preview), warm-started from it. Privileged inputs stay in simulation; the actor sees proprioception and the position target only.</p>
<p><strong>Reward and force cap.</strong> The step reward of report D3 (bounded tracking kernels with the 20 mrad half-width, light torque-rate penalty, contact-force and force-rate penalties, weak restoring term) summed over the 10 held steps; the contact-force penalty starts at 20 N instead of 30. A force reflex models the real robot's collision reaction: when the normal contact force exceeds 40 N the window ends for that robot with a penalty of 5 reward units (a window of perfect tracking is worth about 26), the residual is switched off and the remaining steps are excluded from the update.</p>
<p><strong>PPO.</strong> Generalised advantage estimation (discount 0.995 per physics step, lambda 0.95), clipped objective (0.2), 5 epochs of minibatches of 2048 per update of 12,800 decisions, separate Adam optimisers (actor 1e-4, critic 3e-4), gradient clipping 0.5, normalised advantages, bootstrap at the window end. Validation every 20 updates on the 14 fixed cases of the corrected plant; checkpoints kept at every validation.</p>
<p><strong>Arms.</strong> From the warm start on all training families (the stage-2 mixture of D3), from the warm start on the contact families (contact and blocked with a little hold and free), and from scratch on all families (zero residual, standard deviation 0.2, actor learning rate 3e-4).</p>""",
    "extra_sections": [x for x in (TRAINING_SECTION, VIDEO_SECTION) if x] + ([WINS_SECTION] if WINS_SECTION else []),
    "summary_intro": """<p>Protocol v2 on the corrected plant. The reference PD is the shared gain set of D1 and the warm start is the 20 mrad-kernel residual of D3. Every PPO arm is reported from its final checkpoint (300 updates of 64 windows of 2 s, or 200 of 48 windows of 4 s, or 150 of 32 windows of 6 s; the 1 ms-decision arm from its checkpoint at update 159) so that the effect of the fine-tuning is visible; the free-space arm is also the only arm whose validation-selected checkpoint is not the warm start itself and is reported from it. Three seeds of the v3 arm.</p>""",
    "findings": """<ol>
<li><strong>The pipeline works and is cheap.</strong> One update of 64 windows of 2 s (128,000 physics steps) takes 3.6 s on the laptop when it runs alone; 300 updates, 38 million physics steps, take 18 minutes. No finite differences, no learned model. Fourteen arms were trained in one afternoon.</li>
<li><strong>PPO improves what it is rewarded for, and the free-space arm improves every family it trains on.</strong> The deterministic policy's return on fixed training windows rises from 8.2 to 9.2 over 120 updates for the all-family arm. The free-space arm, whose windows contain no contact, improves hold, free-motion, policy-like and step composites on the validation cases (1.55 to 1.41, 0.62 to 0.52, 1.52 to 1.48, 2.93 to 2.26 at update 24; best mean 3.84 at update 74 against 3.96 for the warm start) while forgetting the contact families it does not see. The optimiser is not the limitation.</li>
<li><strong>On the test set the contact-trained arms end at parity with the warm start, and the contact-family arms move along the warm start's trade-off rather than off it.</strong> The all-family arms end between 2.73 and 2.98 on the in-distribution composite against 2.75 for the warm start (the three seeds of v3: 2.73, 2.80, 2.98), with excess force 3.2 to 3.4 N against 3.6 N. The two contact-family arms cut the forces by a factor of two to four on every contact family (contact family: excess force 0.23 N and peak 36 N against 1.07 N and 80 N; blocked target: excess 15.2 N against 23.3 N; task: peak 22 N against 37 N) and pay with more contact losses (34 against 19 per episode), a larger press deficit (1.44 against 0.65 on the contact family, 2.5 against 1.45 on the task family) and worse free motion (composite 1.39 against 0.71). No arm changed the recovery after a blocked target is released (1.24 to 1.52 s for every learned controller against 0.67 s for the PD). The validation cases, two per family, had ranked every contact-trained arm below the warm start and showed the blocked composite worsening in every variant; on the 16 test cases per family the blocked composite of the better contact-family arm is 6.17 against 6.94 for the warm start and 7.10 for the PD. Two validation cases per family are not enough to select on the contact families.</li>
<li><strong>Five causes were identified and tested in sequence, each by an arm.</strong> (a) The instantaneous 40 N reflex fires on impact transients: 87% of contact windows exceed 40 N for a median of 30 ms, the physics of a blind approach at the reference speed; a reflex on the 20 ms filtered force fires in 15 to 27% of windows instead of 23 to 45%. (b) A reflex that ends the window never trains the behaviour after the impact, which is what the protocol scores; keeping the window and charging the penalty per crossing improved the contact composite (8.65) but not blocked or task. (c) A force penalty from 20 N contradicts the protocol's 30 N press and is charged as press deficit; it is back at 30 N (the cap question is raised for the advisor). (d) The 2 s windows rarely contain the release of a blocked target and the task family is not trained, so the policy drifts in states its windows do not visit: 4 s windows with a drift penalty to the warm start (v3) held the validation composite near the warm start longest, and training on the task family with 6 s windows (full) did not recover the task composite. (e) The feasible-direction kernel as first written removed the reward for pressing toward a target below the surface; rewarding the normal direction up to the 30 N cap (v4) brings the task composite back to the warm start's (2.04 to 2.10 through update 39) without improving blocked targets.</li>
<li><strong>What the blocked family needs is not in the signal.</strong> In a blocked window the position kernel is saturated, the feasible kernel rewards tangential motion that the blocked references barely contain, and the force terms reward only less force; nothing rewards the specific behaviour the protocol scores (yielding at the cap, keeping the tangential error small, re-tracking promptly at release), and the advantage of a single 10 ms action on a 2 s window return is small against the variance of windows drawn from six families, instances and payloads. The first-order methods of D3 got their blocked compliance from the same force terms through a dense gradient; PPO needs either a reward that names the behaviour or far more samples. Evaluating the training reward on saved test episodes separates the two failure modes: on a blocked episode the reward already prefers yielding (the force penalty of a 62 N push costs 31 to 39 units against a saturated position kernel) and neither optimiser finds it, an optimisation problem; on a task episode the reward prefers the residual's light, intermittent contact over the PD's steady 22 N press (98 against 58 units), a reward problem.</li>
<li><strong>Decision period and warm start.</strong> On the warm start itself the period makes no difference between 1 and 20 ms. Under PPO, 1 ms decisions keep the validation composite closest to the warm start (3.97 to 4.13 through update 79) but learn slowest; 5 and 10 ms decisions drift further (4.7). From scratch, the same architecture reaches a training return of 6.9 after 120 updates (from −5) and a validation composite of 4.2 to 5.3 against 3.51 for the PD it starts as: the warm start is worth more than 38 million physics steps of PPO.</li>
<li><strong>What every learned controller still lacks is contact maintenance.</strong> Across the residual and all sixteen PPO arms, the three metrics that reward staying in contact are worse than the PD's in every group: contact losses of 5 ms or more (19 to 43 per contact episode against 3), the press deficit on the task family (1.1 to 2.5 against 0.24) and the recovery after a blocked release (1.2 to 1.5 s against 0.67 s). The saved episodes show the mechanism of the losses: the residual leaves the surface for 5 to 10 ms about thirty times a second, and each loss starts when the normal force reaches 20 to 35 N, at the reward's 30 N soft limit; the PD base spring then pushes the tool back in. It is a learned flinch, not a tracking failure, and the decision period does not suppress it (the warm start's contact composite is the same at 1, 5, 10 and 20 ms).</li>
</ol>""",
    "caveats": """<ul>
<li>One seed per arm except v3 (three). The 14 validation cases (two per family) ranked the contact-trained arms differently from the 16 test cases per family (finding 3); the test tables are the evidence for the final checkpoints, and the training curves should be read as a screen.</li>
<li>The composite is the selection objective, not a research signal; the per-metric tables follow the summary.</li>
<li>The feasible-direction kernel and the critic use simulation-only information (surface normal, touch force, payload, reference preview); the actor does not. The full and v4full arms train on the task family, which the protocol otherwise holds out; their task rows are therefore not a generalisation result.</li>
<li>The reflex is a training device modelling the real robot's collision reaction; the evaluation does not stop the robot, it reports the share of episodes whose sustained (20 ms filtered) force exceeds 40 N.</li>
<li>Budget: 38 to 50 million physics steps per arm, a few hours of laptop time; the blind-assembly and blind-dexterity results this work follows used orders of magnitude more samples on clusters.</li>
</ul>""",
    "next": """<ul>
<li>Diagnostics before training: the training reward evaluated on every contact test episode of the PD, the residual and the PPO arms (where the reward and the protocol disagree); the chatter and the recovery mechanisms from the saved episodes; eight validation cases per contact family.</li>
<li>A reward that names contact maintenance, shared by both training methods: a kernel on the normal force around the force implied by the commanded depth, capped, while the reference is inside the surface; a charge for losing contact during press phases; force-rate damping that sees small events. All from privileged simulation quantities; the actor stays proprioceptive.</li>
<li>A variable-gain residual: bounded per-joint multipliers on the PD gains plus a smaller torque residual, warm-started to reproduce the current residual, so that yielding and holding are small, contact-stable actions.</li>
<li>Training windows that start in the press phase and windows that contain the release; for PPO, temporally correlated exploration and an external-torque estimate from the robot's own model as an input (the signal the Panda provides on hardware); bespoke contact and blocked arms with three seeds, then the mixture; a privileged teacher only if these fail.</li>
<li>The force cap and the press target remain a decision for the advisor.</li>
</ul>""",
}
